# Line endings for the scripts that run via shebang on Linux.
#
# Without this file, git on Windows can check these out with CRLF. A shell or
# python script with CRLF then fails on Linux in a way that does not resemble
# its cause: the kernel looks for an interpreter literally named "bash\r" or
# "python3\r" and reports that it does not exist. Nothing in the error mentions
# line endings.
#
# We lost half a day to exactly this on 11-Sep-2026 with a script uploaded to
# the server: nine checks red and five FALSELY GREEN, because a command that
# never runs makes every "this is not present" assertion true.
#
# Every path below is already LF in the repository, so this protects them
# without rewriting anything.
scripts/eva-app-ctl        text eol=lf
scripts/eva-app-idle       text eol=lf
scripts/eva-backup.py      text eol=lf
scripts/instalar.sh        text eol=lf
scripts/participante.py    text eol=lf
tests/install_check.sh     text eol=lf
examples/client.py         text eol=lf
modulos/**/*.sh            text eol=lf
modulos/**/*.py            text eol=lf
caddy/*                    text eol=lf
systemd/*                  text eol=lf

# NOT listed on purpose: server.py, scripts/tools_reference.py and
# tests/battery.py. They carry a shebang but are always invoked with an explicit
# interpreter (`python server.py`), and they are stored with CRLF today. Forcing
# eol=lf would rewrite all three in full, turning a line-ending policy into a
# four-thousand-line diff that buries the history of the file. Normalising them
# is a deliberate change of its own, not a side effect of this one.

# Binaries: keep git away from them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
